  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Mendl Mann’s <i>The Fall of Berlin</i> shows strong signs of active readership, with 5,129 recorded downloads and broader open-access availability through library and publisher platforms. The scholarly citation record is still at zero, so the current impact profile is centered on use and access rather than formal academic uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly edition of Mendl Mann’s autobiographical novel has reached readers through a clearly visible access network. The record shows open availability on repository and publisher platforms, alongside substantial downloading activity and additional visibility through Google Books views. Taken together, these signals indicate that the book is circulating and being consulted, even though the citation record has not yet developed.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is usage. The work has accumulated 5,129 downloads, with activity distributed across Open Book Publishers, OAPEN, and Google Books. That pattern suggests sustained interest in the text across multiple channels rather than a single isolated spike, and it is especially notable for a historical literary work made openly available online.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The wider record is quieter on scholarly and public discourse. OpenAlex currently shows no citations, and the archive data do not preserve event coverage for this title. That means there is little evidence here for formal academic referencing or documented public events, but it does not diminish the usage evidence already visible in the download and access metrics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s subject matter and framing help explain its relevance: it presents a Jewish soldier’s experience in the Red Army during the destruction of Poland’s Jewish communities, and the translation makes that testimony available to an English-language readership. In the present record, the book’s impact is best understood as access-driven and reader-centered, with signs of adoption through library holdings and sustained online consultation rather than through citation-based scholarly accumulation.</p>",
